\import{macros}
\import{lean-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\title{Computer solving and efficiency}

\p{A computer solver answers a sharper question than a human method: from a
recorded state, what is the shortest path to solved, or what short path can be
found quickly? The answer depends on the move metric, the state coordinates,
and the information retained by the search. We use the fixed-frame ordinary
3-layer action from [the complete cube account](frap-0016), with the six outer
face turns as generators. The [methods chapter](frap-0005) supplies the
group-action graph viewpoints; here they become finite search procedures.}

\subtree{
  \title{Move graphs and lower bounds}

  \p{For a move metric #{M}, let #{\Gamma_M} be the undirected graph whose
  vertices are legal cube states and whose edges apply one allowed move, with
  each edge weighted by that move's cost in #{M}. A
  \newvocab{graph distance} is the function}
  ##{d_M(x,y)=\text{the minimum total edge weight on a path from }x\text{ to }y.}
  \p{The metric diameter is the maximum of #{d_M(x,y)} over legal states; in
  a transitive move graph it is also the maximum distance from the solved state.
  For unit-cost moves, breadth-first search from solved labels every reachable
  vertex by exact distance, because the first time a vertex is reached there is
  no shorter path left in the earlier layers. With non-unit weights, Dijkstra's
  algorithm supplies the analogous exact labels. A depth-first method can use
  the same graph without storing all of it; optimal branch-and-bound and IDA*
  need a certified lower bound to prune safely.}

  \p{The usual half-turn metric (HTM) charges each face quarter-turn,
  inverse quarter-turn, or half-turn one move. The quarter-turn metric (QTM)
  charges a half-turn two moves. A word can therefore be shorter in HTM while
  representing the same group element; an efficiency claim is incomplete
  until its metric and move set are named.}

  \p{A simple counting bound already separates search from proof. In HTM
  there are at most eighteen first moves and at most seventeen choices after
  the first if an immediate inverse is suppressed. Hence a ball of radius
  #{r} has at most}
  ##{1+18\sum_{i=0}^{r-1}17^i}
  \p{vertices. The reachable 3-layer state count from [the complete cube
  account](frap-0016) is}
  ##{N_3=8!\,12!\,3^7\,2^{10}
      =43\,252\,003\,274\,489\,856\,000.}
  \p{The corresponding upper estimate for a radius-15 ball is}
  ##{1+18\sum_{i=0}^{14}17^i
      =3\,220\,225\,932\,948\,542\,767<N_3.}
  \p{Thus not every state can lie within fifteen moves, so the HTM diameter
  is at least #{16}. This is only a counting lower bound: it does not exhibit
  a state at distance sixteen, and it says nothing about a particular
  scramble.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{One exact small search}

  \p{The same procedure can be made exhaustive on the corner-only cube. In a
  fixed-frame labelling (there are no centers on a #{2\times2\times2}), a
  legal state is a corner permutation together with seven independent twists,
  so there are}
  ##{8!\,3^7=88\,179\,840}
  \p{labelled states. Quotienting by the twenty-four orientations of the
  whole cube leaves #{3\,674\,160} rotation classes. Enumerate the six face
  quarter-turns, their inverses, and the six face half-turns; canonicalize
  each state by the whole-cube rotation action, and keep the first depth at
  which each rotation class is seen. The resulting layer sizes in HTM are}
  ##{1,9,54,321,1847,9992,50136,227536,
      870072,1887748,623800,2644.}
  \p{They sum to #{3\,674\,160}; the last nonempty layer is #{11}. Thus
  this finite breadth-first computation proves that the rotation-quotient
  corner-only cube has diameter eleven in HTM. It is an \newvocab{exact
  search}: every rotation class is covered and every recorded depth is a
  shortest distance. The calculation
  agrees with the published exhaustive layer table
  [for the pocket cube, p. 3](https://www.cs.brandeis.edu/~storer/JimPuzzles/RUBIK/Rubik2x2x2/Rubik2x2x2SolutionJaap.pdf). The calculation also
  illustrates why the full 3-layer graph is not attacked by naïve BFS: its
  state count is larger by many orders of magnitude.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Admissible heuristics and pattern databases}

  \p{A \newvocab{projection heuristic} forgets part of a state. If #{\pi}
  maps the full move graph to a smaller graph and every full move projects to
  a projected path of no greater total cost, then}
  ##{h_\pi(x)=d_{\pi}(\pi(x),\pi(\mathrm{id}))}
  \p{never overestimates the full distance: projecting a full solution gives
  a projected solution of no greater cost. For example, a table entry
  #{h_\pi(x)=2} certifies that every full solution from #{x} costs at least
  two. Such a function is an \newvocab{admissible heuristic}. A maximum of
  admissible heuristics is admissible; a sum needs an additional cost-partition
  argument and is not automatically safe. The graph maps here are specified by
  these equations and move lists, so a separate vector diagram would add no
  state or cost data.}

  \p{A \newvocab{pattern database} stores these projected distances in a
  table indexed by a selected set of cubies or coordinates. Reverse
  breadth-first search fills the table once; an iterative-deepening A*
  (IDA*) search then explores depth thresholds while pruning a node #{x}
  whenever #{g+h_\pi(x)} exceeds
  the threshold. Korf's Rubik's-cube computation used this combination
  of pattern databases and IDA* for optimal solutions to random instances
  [the AAAI paper, abstract and sections “Problem Space” and “Pattern Databases”](https://aaai.org/Papers/AAAI/1997/AAAI97-109.pdf).
  The table certifies a lower bound, while the exhaustive threshold search
  is what certifies optimality.}

  \p{For the 3-layer coordinates, a corner-orientation table has #{3^7}
  entries, an edge-orientation table has #{2^{11}}, and a chosen edge-position
  table can be indexed separately. Taking the maximum of their exact
  projected distances is safe and often much stronger than either table
  alone. Memory, lookup cost, and the number of expanded nodes are measurable
  resources; none of them changes the definition of the shortest distance.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Subgroup reduction and two phases}

  \p{A different compression searches through a \newvocab{goal subgroup}.
  Let}
  ##{G_1=\langle U,D,R^2,L^2,F^2,B^2\rangle.}
  \p{In this subgroup the corner and edge orientations are fixed, and the
  four middle-slice edges stay in their slice. A first phase searches the
  full generator set for a word taking the scramble into #{G_1}; a second
  phase solves inside #{G_1} using only its generators. The phase-1
  coordinates are corner orientation, edge orientation, and the choice of
  the four slice edges, with raw size}
  ##{3^7\,2^{11}\binom{12}{4}=2\,217\,093\,120.}
  \p{Pruning tables and symmetry reductions store only useful projections of
  this space.}

  \p{Kociemba's two-phase account describes IDA* over these coordinates and
  explicitly distinguishes a fast near-optimal solver from an optimal solver
  [the Phase 1, Phase 2 and optimality paragraphs](https://www.kociemba.org/twophase.htm). A
  solution found by one phase-1 word followed by a phase-2 word is a valid
  upper bound. It becomes a proof of optimality only when every shorter
  possibility under the declared metric has been excluded. Restricting phase
  two to #{G_1} makes the search practical, but can omit an overall shortest
  route that leaves and later re-enters #{G_1}.}

  \p{This reduction is the computer analogue of the preservation chain in
  [the complete layer-first method](frap-0017): both deliberately fix
  information before solving what remains. The difference is the certificate.
  A human stage records a finite case argument; a search stage records a
  lower bound, an upper-bound word, and the unexplored threshold.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Efficiency and growing cubes}

  \p{A useful report separates four measurements: solution length in a named
  metric, number of expanded nodes, peak table memory, and wall-clock time on
  named hardware. Recognition and move execution belong to the human method;
  node expansions and table construction belong to the computer method. A
  shorter returned word is evidence of a better upper bound, not by itself a
  proof that the word is shortest.}

  \p{For the growing #{N\times N\times N} family, the asymptotic question is
  how the diameter changes with #{N} when a move rotates one legal slice.
  Demaine, Demaine, Eisenstat, Lubiw and Winslow prove a scoped result for
  this family: the diameter is #{\Theta(N^2/\log N)}, with a counting lower
  bound and a parallelized upper-bound construction
  [their abstract and Section 2, “Common Definitions”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.5736).
  Their model allows a legal slice rotation as defined there; that move
  convention must be checked before transferring the statement to a
  different layered-cube convention.}

  \p{The fixed-frame 3-layer count and the exact corner-only search give two
  finite anchors for that asymptotic discussion. The asymptotic theorem does
  not make a particular 3-layer word optimal, and the exact small search does
  not predict the table size or diameter of larger cubes. Across all sizes,
  correctness means reaching the target; optimality means proving a shortest
  path; efficiency means reporting the resources used to obtain either one.}
}

\p{Computer solving therefore extends the earlier construction in three
controlled directions: exact graph distances on a small quotient, admissible
lower bounds for the full state space, and subgroup reductions that trade
completeness of the search for speed. These distinctions let a later return to
play report a solver's output without confusing a valid solve, a short solve,
and a certified optimum.}

\p{This chapter is a mathematical specification, not a Lean-certified solver.
For comparison, [rubik-lean4 at commit b8a5a09](https://github.com/vihdzp/rubik-lean4/tree/b8a5a09)
formalizes the fixed-frame move and sticker correspondence, while
[rubiks-cube-group at commit 3baebd73](https://github.com/kendfrey/rubiks-cube-group/tree/3baebd73)
formalizes the legal-state subgroup; neither repository is used here as a
formal proof of the search, diameter, or optimality claims above.}
